#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074610} :activity/id #uuid "d1e56cd5-fc3b-5ec2-ae90-20ca4a2e732f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050665 :topic/id #uuid "5d634c2c-a4e7-591a-891e-89c65f30d787" :topic/math-academy-id 529} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075043 :tutorial/content "Remember that a **unit fraction** is a fraction containing $1$ in the numerator.\n\nFor example, the following fractions are unit fractions since they each contain ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{2}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{5}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{8}\n$$\n\nHowever, the following fractions are *not* unit fractions since they do *not* contain ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}{2}}{3}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{5}}{8}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{7}}{12}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will use models to divide whole numbers by unit fractions.\n\nLet's start with an example. Suppose we divide the pizza below into fifths. How many fifths will there be in total?\n\n![](images/4a/4a73488a784067fbe0a4281ef8de45209e284bcd8675f5476c14a8bdcb357712.png)\n\nSince each part is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nof a whole, we need to split $\\color{red}1$ pizza (whole) into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e36c7b607bd6ada0018e574d52c060851837b62025429b6cfb94f98ed378ccd.png)\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}1} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 5\n$$\n\nparts in total." :tutorial/id #uuid "577d7682-3ed8-5103-8641-0c02702a9e08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8557 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "26245a65-ebc8-5e42-9300-6d874c79a4ec" :step/math-academy-id 172318 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074611}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073461 :question/id #uuid "4f8f2348-c6ec-5073-9f95-6697173654a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13690"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2b57f76-8fab-5b44-80ee-d905e5f9a14f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049517}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074521}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining How Many Parts Fit Into a Whole"} :step/id #uuid "3c05bf1b-ab93-581a-9e20-32a418b2cda1" :step/math-academy-id 172319 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075044 :tutorial/content "The model below represents $2$ wholes. Let's use it to calculate the value of\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n![](images/bc/bceafee7d9ca5e92e69b5d33d9504f4290526b80059ad62ed26229d02712a278.png)\n\nTo calculate\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\n, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}2$ wholes into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf608ce8f5d8e2a47a4bebe21f198d193b10dd8f6e95436986bc954c89c60d19.png)\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 10\n$$\n\npieces in total.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5} = 10\\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2de869cb-775d-5db4-b90d-7df0de218fc0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8561 :tutorial/title "Dividing Whole Numbers by Unit Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "480191b5-d5a1-50b4-8e0c-c36a3b10da79" :step/math-academy-id 172323 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073721 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073462 :question/id #uuid "2f739b94-f494-546b-8623-bd17a525a436" :question/math-academy-id "e-13695"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed8053e5-f273-56f2-bfb6-294f936d9701" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074386}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Model Representing a Division Problem"} :step/id #uuid "242be67e-027e-54a4-9866-c63cb081faa9" :step/math-academy-id 172324 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074614}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073722 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073467 :question/id #uuid "dd5b4bc8-110c-5247-b5e6-ee4ce7636a1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13707"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e33ab8e-736b-5972-af0d-f7eeabda1148" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074366}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Division Problem Represented by a Model"} :step/id #uuid "7721ecc8-af47-5723-b955-d68c3dc4eecb" :step/math-academy-id 172327 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073723 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073468 :question/id #uuid "06e730e3-2df3-5d28-afd1-72c34d11cf8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13709"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79e310e4-b8a2-5327-b4e6-fd71b6783e3a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424809}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Whole Numbers by Unit Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "b662cd66-040d-56ae-be80-ddf72c03e975" :step/math-academy-id 172330}] :activity/title "Dividing Whole Numbers by Unit Fractions Using Models" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073949}]}
